---
title: 'Candidate Rediscovery: How to Turn Old Resumes and Spreadsheets Into a Searchable Talent Pool'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** Candidate rediscovery means finding strong people you've already met (past applicants, silver-medal finalists, referrals and event contacts) when a new role opens, instead of starting from zero. To do it, gather your old resumes and spreadsheets in one place, structure them (name, contact, title, skills, location), make them searchable by meaning rather than exact keywords, and match them against each new job. On GetHired, you can import resumes or a spreadsheet as company-private candidates, and each new job shows a "From your candidates" list ranked by similarity.

Every startup collects candidates over time: last year's applicants, the finalist who just missed out, a friend's referral, contacts from a meetup. Most of them end up scattered across inboxes, spreadsheets and download folders. When a new role opens, the team posts a job and starts again, while some of the best candidates are already sitting in a folder.

## Why candidate rediscovery works

- **You already know them.** Past finalists have passed some of your interviews.
- **They already know you.** Response rates are usually higher than for cold outreach.
- **It's faster.** No job ads, no waiting for applicants.
- **It's cheaper.** You're reusing work you've already paid for.

Silver medallists, the runner-up candidates for a role, are especially valuable. Many would have been a great hire if you'd had two positions.

## Step 1: Gather everything in one place

Common sources of past candidates:

- Resumes in email attachments and shared drives.
- Spreadsheets from earlier hiring rounds.
- Your old ATS or job board exports.
- Referral lists and event sign-ups.
- Notes from founders' networks.

Before you import anything, check that you have a lawful basis to keep and use each person's data, and remove anyone who asked to be deleted.

## Step 2: Structure the data

A searchable pool needs consistent fields:

{% table %}
- Field
- Why it matters
---
- Name and email
- To contact them
---
- Current title and company
- Quick context
---
- Years of experience
- Rough seniority
---
- Location
- For location-based roles
---
- Skills
- For matching
---
- Resume text
- The richest source for matching
---
- Source and date added
- To know how fresh the record is
---
- Tags and notes
- Your team's context
{% /table %}

Doing this by hand for hundreds of resumes isn't realistic. AI resume parsing can extract these fields automatically, which you then check.

## Step 3: Make it searchable by meaning

Keyword search misses a lot. A resume that says "built event-driven microservices with Kafka" won't match a search for "streaming data engineer", even though the person is a good fit.

**Semantic search** uses embeddings: numerical representations of meaning, so that similar experience matches similar requirements even with different words. Read more about [how vector search works in candidate screening](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/beyond-keywords-how-vector-search-and-ai-are-revolutionizing-candidate-screening).

## Step 4: Match the pool against every new job

When you open a role, check your pool first:

1. Rank existing candidates by how similar they are to the job.
2. Review the top matches and their notes.
3. Reach out to the strongest ones with a personal message that mentions your history. Our [outreach templates](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/recruiting-outreach-messages-that-get-replies-8-templates-for-tech-candidates) include one for past applicants.
4. Add those who are interested to the job's pipeline.

## Step 5: Keep the pool fresh

- Add every new finalist and referral as you go.
- Tag people by skill area and seniority.
- Leave notes on why someone was a good near-miss.
- Delete records you no longer have a reason to keep.

## How to do it on GetHired

GetHired lets you build a company-private candidate pool from your own data and match it against every job.

### Import your candidates three ways

- **Resumes**: upload a batch of resume files. GetHired reads each one with AI and extracts the name, contact details, title, experience, skills and location for you to review.
- **Spreadsheet**: upload a CSV or Excel file and map its columns.
- **Form**: add one person at a time.

You choose where they go: your candidates only, a talent list, or straight into a job's pipeline. These records are private to your company and never linked to anyone's GetHired account automatically.

### Search by meaning

Each imported candidate gets an embedding built from their title, company, years of experience, location, skills and resume. It **never includes their name, email, phone, links, tags or notes**, so matching is based on experience, not identity.

The Candidates page lets you search and filter by text, location, whether they have a resume or email, date added, and "Match to job".

### See matches for every job

Each job has a **From your candidates** section that ranks your pool by similarity to the job, and leaves out anyone already in that job's pipeline. When you create a new job, GetHired points you to it. Each candidate's page also shows **Matching jobs** across your open roles.

Similarity is shown as a 0–100 score labelled "Similarity", a measure of how close their experience is to the job description, not a judgement of fit.

### Add them to a job, without surprise emails

Adding pool candidates to a job never emails them. When you do contact them, every email carries an unsubscribe link, and anyone who opts out isn't emailed again.

### Or ask the recruiter agent

Ask the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat): "Find people in our candidates who'd fit the new platform role." It searches your pool and, with your approval, adds them to the job.

### When imported candidates join GetHired

If an imported candidate is invited to an [AI screening interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-screening-interviews-replace-30-phone-screens-with-one-page-reports), they get a secure link to claim their application with a GetHired account. If they've also applied to the same job themselves, the two applications are merged so you don't see duplicates.

## Privacy and consent

Candidate data is personal data. Good practice:

- Keep records only while you have a lawful reason to.
- Tell candidates how you'll use their information.
- Honour deletion and opt-out requests promptly.
- Limit who on your team can see and import candidates. On GetHired, importing needs the `applicants.import` permission, which you assign through [custom roles](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/hiring-as-a-team-custom-roles-permissions-and-hiring-teams-on-gethired).

When you import candidates on GetHired, confirming the import is your statement that you have a lawful basis to process their data.

## Frequently asked questions

### What is candidate rediscovery?

It's the practice of searching candidates you already have, such as past applicants and finalists, when a new role opens, rather than only sourcing new people.

### What is a silver medallist candidate?

A candidate who reached the final stage of a hiring process but wasn't hired, often because another strong candidate was chosen. They're among the best people to contact for future roles.

### How long should I keep candidate data?

Only as long as you have a lawful reason and the candidate would reasonably expect. Many companies keep it for one to two years and ask candidates for consent to keep it longer.

### Can I import resumes from my old ATS?

Usually yes. Export the resumes or a spreadsheet from your old system, then import them. On GetHired you can upload resume files or a spreadsheet.

### Does a similarity score mean a candidate is qualified?

No. It shows how close their experience is to the job description. Review each person yourself before reaching out.

